  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Turkish moon names and the word ay",
      "content": "<p>A lot of the names here come from Turkish and the neighbouring languages, where <em>ay</em> means moon. It turns up at the start (Aylin, Aysel, Ayhan) and at the end (Nuray, Berkay, Koray), joined to words for light, purity, crowns and luck. If you like the sound, you can read most of these names by spotting the <em>ay</em>.</p><p>One catch: Ayla is two different names. The Turkish one means moonlight or halo; the Hebrew one means oak tree. Both are used in England and Wales, so if the meaning matters to you, say which one you mean.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Spelling and pronunciation",
      "content": "<p>Several of these names arrive through other alphabets, so there's more than one way to write them. Chandra is also Candra, and Qamar is also Kamar. Pick the spelling that's established in the family or community the name comes from, rather than one that just looks good in English.</p><p>Japanese names such as Mitsuki and Tsukiko only mean moon when they're written with the moon kanji 月. The same sounds can be written with other characters and mean something else entirely.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Pairing a moon name with a surname",
      "content": "<p>Short names like Luna, Hala and Qamar sit well with long surnames, while longer ones like Chandrashekhar or Purnima suit a short surname. Watch the join: Luna Andrews runs the two A sounds together, and Selene Lee doubles the 'ee' sound. Saying the full name aloud a few times is still the best test.</p>"
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What girl names mean moon?",
      "answer": "Luna (Latin) and Selene (Greek) are the best known. Others include Aylin, Aysel and Aynur (Turkish), Mahsa and Mahtab (Persian), Mahina (Hawaiian), Purnima (Sanskrit, 'full moon') and Levana (Hebrew)."
    },
    {
      "question": "What boy names mean moon?",
      "answer": "Badr means full moon and Hilal crescent moon in Arabic. Ayhan, Koray and Kutay are Turkish names built on ay, 'moon', and Rakesh means 'lord of the full moon' in Sanskrit. Chandra and Qamar, 'moon' itself, are used for boys and girls."
    },
    {
      "question": "What name means moon in Japanese?",
      "answer": "Names written with the kanji 月 (tsuki, moon), such as Tsukiko ('moon child') and Mitsuki ('beautiful moon'). The same names can be written with other kanji, so the meaning depends on the characters chosen."
    }
  ],
